explanation:
The body applies the library's generic `splitKernelProductIso` to the upper-triangular data. In a cartesian monoidal category, that construction takes a group object G, a kernel inclusion i : N → G (a pullback of the unit along q : G → Q) and a section e of q. Its forward map is `(snd ≫ e) * (fst ≫ i)`, which is (v, d) ↦ e(d)·i(v) on points. Its inverse is the pair (remainder, q).

Here the data are:
- N is the unitriangular scheme U;
- Q is the diagonal scheme D;
- i is the inclusion of U into the upper-triangular scheme B;
- q is the diagonal projection B → D;
- e is the diagonal section D → B.

The pullback hypothesis says U is the kernel of q, and `upperTriangularSection_over` says e ≫ q = id. So the definition is exactly the stated map F(v, d) = e(d)·i(v). Mathematically this is the standard isomorphism of underlying schemes U × D ≅ B coming from B = T ⋉ U, written with the diagonal factor on the left. The docstring is explicit about this convention, and the module separately builds the U-first variant `upperTriangularUFirstIso` from it.

The generality is natural: K is an arbitrary commutative ring and n is an arbitrary finite linearly ordered index type, which is what defining upper-triangular matrices needs. The isomorphism is not claimed to be an isomorphism of group objects, and it isn't one, which is consistent with the docstring. Mathlib has no group schemes of triangular matrices and no such decomposition isomorphism, so there is no Mathlib counterpart.
